      "Two words on the menu carry the promise. Da Nang houses advertise <em>sạch gàu</em>, clean of dandruff, while Bliss Spa's own service page tells customers that a herbal wash clears dirt and sebum off the scalp and keeps dandruff from coming back. The Vietnamese listing sites that aggregate the trade go further and credit individual houses with treating <em>gàu</em>, itching and hair fall. None of them cites a study. There is also no trial on gội đầu dưỡng sinh and flakes that they could cite.",
      "The research describes a different problem. Reviews published through 2025 place the yeast genus <em>Malassezia</em> at the centre of dandruff and seborrhoeic dermatitis. It is lipid-dependent, and its lipases break the triglycerides in sebum into free fatty acids, which irritate the skin and drive inflammation. According to the Merck Manual, sebum composition and flow are usually normal in these patients, and a 2025 review in the International Journal of Molecular Sciences argues that the yeast is not the direct cause on its own, bringing barrier and immune factors into the frame. Dirt does not appear in the frame at all.",
